      "text": "To find the number of distinct numbers in the list \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{3^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\ \\dots, \\ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\), we will first simplify the general term of the sequence. The \\(n\\)-th term in the sequence is given by:\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\nWe need to determine the distinct values of this sequence as \\(n\\) ranges from 1 to 1000. To do this, we will identify the range of values each term can take and count the distinct results.\n\n1. **Identify the range for each term:**\n   - For \\(n = 1\\), the term is \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 0\\).\n   - For \\(n = 10\\), the term is \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{10^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 1\\).\n   - For \\(n = 100\\), the term is \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{100^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 100\\).\n   - For \\(n = 1000\\), the term is \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 1000\\).\n\n   As \\(n\\) increases, the value of \\(\\frac{n^2}{1000}\\) increases and the floor function will take on values from 0 to the integer part of \\(100n\\). However, not all integer values in this range are necessarily achievable because \\(\\frac{n^2}{1000}\\) is an integer only when \\(n\\) divides 1000.\n\n2. **Determine the integer part of \\(100n\\):**\n   The integer part of \\(100n\\) is easy to understand because \\(100n\\) is always an integer for integer \\(n\\). The distinct integers in the range from 0 to \\(1000\\) that are achieved are just 0, 1, 2, ..., 1000. However, we must exclude the integer part of \\(\\frac{1000}{100} = 10\\) because \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{10^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 1\\).\n\n   Therefore, the distinct values are 0, 1, 2, ..., 9, 10, 20, 30, ..., 1000, minus the value for \\(n = 10\\) because \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{10^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 1\\).\n\n3. **Count the distinct values:**\n   The distinct values are the integers from 0 to 999, which gives us \\(999 - 0 + 1 = 999\\) distinct values.\n\nThus, the number of distinct numbers in the list is \\(\\boxed{999}\\).",
